WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
It's May 18th, 2026. New week, new discoveries. Let's see what's happening.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:11.760
A faint crescent moon is sliding past Venus tonight, offering a rare chance to watch two worlds share

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.240
the same patch of sky. The slender crescent moon stands just over two days old and will appear close

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.280
to Venus this evening. Observers can catch the pairing low in the west after sunset as the moon's

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:31.200
sliver grows night by night. While ground observers enjoy this quiet celestial moment,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.820
crews on the station are catching far more dramatic views of our own atmosphere.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.840
An astronaut aboard the International Space Station captured a sudden shower of bright streaks

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.140
while gazing through the cupola's windows. The image shows objects streaking across the darkness

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:53.240
of Earth's night side, likely meteors or small pieces of debris heating up as they encounter the

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:59.380
thin upper atmosphere. Such views from orbit give scientists a unique vantage point above the

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:05.180
weather and city lights that often obscure ground observations. From one privileged view of Earth,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.880
we turn to the people who earned their place in the history books. Tom Acres and Joe Tanner were

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.180
inducted at Kennedy Space Center Visitor Complex for their contributions to shuttle missions and

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:22.520
spacewalks. The ceremony recognizes decades of service that advanced NASA's human spaceflight

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:29.260
program. The same spirit of pushing boundaries now shows up in new commercial alliances racing to

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:36.480
match Starlink. Verizon, AT&amp;T and T-Mobile have joined forces for the first time to develop direct

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.160
to device satellite service. The move responds to Starlink's expanding coverage that threatens

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.000
traditional ground networks. While established players consolidate, a Chinese startup is taking

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.940
its own first step toward orbit. The company raised 180 million yuan to complete development of

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:02.220
its G-hying one, kerosene liquid oxygen rocket. A debut launch attempt is now planned for June from

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:08.280
a site in China. New rockets keep coming because decades of spacework keep feeding practical advances

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.580
back on Earth. Decades of work on propulsion, materials, and life support have produced spin-offs

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:21.080
now used in medicine, transportation, and consumer electronics. The article traces how solving

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:27.100
extreme environment challenges on orbit leads to practical innovations on Earth. One of the most

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:32.820
visible symbols of that engineering legacy was the simple way NASA moved its shuttles across the

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:39.940
country. NASA used a specially adapted Boeing 747 to ferry orbiters between landing sites and

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:46.140
launch pads. The arrangement led the shuttle glide home to Florida or California without burning

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:52.580
its own engines for cross-country trips. Space keeps offering new angles on familiar hardware and

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:58.220
fresh views of our own planet. Now here's something that blew my mind when I looked into it.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.180
If the sun were scaled to the size of a basketball sitting in downtown Toronto, the sphere of our

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:09.560
strongest radio broadcast would already stretch past the nearest star and keep expanding at the speed

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:15.520
of light every second. Picture riding alongside one of those early television signals as it leaves

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.520
the ionosphere. Once free of Earth's protective layers, it travels in a straight line forever,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:28.700
growing fainter with distance yet never truly disappearing. By the time a 1980s era broadcast reaches

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:35.260
a star 50 light years away, it has spread into a shell so vast that detecting it requires an antenna

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:43.080
hundreds of kilometers across. Signals from the 1930s now form an outer shell roughly 240 light

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.900
years in diameter. We can map exactly which nearby stars have already received our earliest

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:55.440
transmissions and which ones will not hear us for centuries yet. The lingering puzzle is whether

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.700
any receiver out there could ever distinguish our faint whispers from the natural radio noise of the

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:06.400
galaxy itself. Keep an eye on developments with commercial satellite networks as more alliances

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:12.900
and new launch attempts take shape. That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:18.200
a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.140
I'm Patrick and Vancouver. Thanks for exploring with me and I'll see you next time.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.060
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
